UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 14, Lesson 2: Active water challenge', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"An active water challenge links movement with hydration: do short bursts of activity, take water breaks and see how the body changes. Plan breaks before you feel very thirsty, drink slowly, stop if you feel dizzy or unwell and rest in the shade on hot days. A challenge can be against yourself, such as keeping your own drink diary for a week, rather than against other people.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA challenge has the rule ''no water breaks until you have finished''. Rewrite the rule to make it safe and say why.\n\nFEEDBACK\nRewrite: ''Take a water break after every round, and anyone can stop to rest or drink at any time.'' Water breaks protect the body, and the choice to stop keeps the challenge safe and inclusive.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving the best athletes ignore thirst. Drinking regularly and resting are part of safe, healthy performance.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPlan and do a safe 15-minute active challenge for yourself or your family with at least three water breaks. Record thirst ratings from 1 to 5 before and after each round and write two conclusions. Adjust the activity for hot weather or any health need.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"An active water challenge links movement with hydration: do short bursts of activity, take water breaks and see how the body changes. Plan breaks before you feel very thirsty, drink slowly, stop if you feel dizzy or unwell and rest in the shade on hot days. A challenge can be against yourself, such as keeping your own drink diary for a week, rather than against other people.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-IHW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Plan and do a safe 15-minute active challenge for yourself or your family with at least three water breaks. Record thirst ratings from 1 to 5 before and after each round and write two conclusions. Adjust the activity for Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Rewrite: ''Take a water break after every round, and anyone can stop to rest or drink at any time.'' Water breaks protect the body, and the choice to stop keeps the challenge safe and inclusive. Reasoning: I used an active challenge by attending to a water break rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Believing the best athletes ignore thirst. Drinking regularly and resting are part of safe, healthy performance. Correction: Rewrite: ''Take a water break after every round, and anyone can stop to rest or drink at any time.'' Water breaks protect the body, and the choice to stop keeps… Why: the correction restores accurate use of an active challenge.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing the best athletes ignore thirst. Drinking regularly and resting are part of safe, healthy performance. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating an active challenge on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with an active challenge, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Rewrite: ''Take a water break after every round, and anyone can stop to rest or drink at any time.'' Water breaks protect the body, and the choice to stop keeps… This matches the lesson skill for an active challenge.","options":["Believing the best athletes ignore thirst. Drinking regularly and resting are part of safe, healthy performance.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Rewrite: ''Take a water break after every round, and anyone can stop to rest or drink at any time.'' Water breaks protect the body, and the choice to stop keeps…"],"question":"A challenge has the rule ''no water breaks until you have finished''. Rewrite the rule to make it safe and say why. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Believing the best athletes ignore thirst. Drinking regularly and resting are part of safe, healthy performance. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of a water break with clear evidence.","Believing the best athletes ignore thirst. Drinking regularly and resting are part of safe, healthy performance.","An active water challenge links movement with hydration: do short bursts of activity, take water breaks and see how the body changes. Plan breaks before you…","Rewrite: ''Take a water break after every round, and anyone can stop to rest or drink at any time.'' Water breaks protect the body, and the choice to stop keeps…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising an active challenge?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named a water break, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the a water break used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising an active challenge. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Rewrite: ''Take a water break after every round, and anyone can stop to rest or drink at any time.'' Water breaks protect the body, and the choice to stop keeps… uses safe, healthy, respectful choices appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Rewrite: ''Take a water break after every round, and anyone can stop to rest or drink at any time.'' Water breaks protect the body, and the choice to stop keeps…","Believing the best athletes ignore thirst. Drinking regularly and resting are part of safe, healthy performance."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about an active challenge?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about an active challenge.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Believing the best athletes ignore thirst. Drinking regularly and resting are part of safe, healthy performance."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about a water break?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example challenge: four rounds of 2 minutes of activity (star jumps, jogging on the spot, hopping, marching), each followed by a 1 minute…","Believing the best athletes ignore thirst. Drinking regularly and resting are part of safe, healthy performance.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — An active water challenge links movement with hydration: do short bursts of activity, take water breaks and… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Plan and do a safe 15-minute active challenge for yourself or your family with at least three water breaks. Record thirst ratings from 1 to 5 before…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Believing the best athletes ignore thirst. Drinking regularly and resting are part of safe, healthy performance."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of an active challenge?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating an active challenge on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Rewrite: ''Take a water break after every round, and anyone can stop to rest or drink at any time.'' Water breaks protect the body, and the choice to stop keeps the challenge safe and inclusive. Reasoning: I used an active challenge by attending to a water break rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with an active challenge, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Believing the best athletes ignore thirst. Drinking regularly and resting are part of safe, healthy performance. Correction: Rewrite: ''Take a water break after every round, and anyone can stop to rest or drink at any time.'' Water breaks protect the body, and the choice to stop keeps… Why: the correction restores accurate use of an active challenge.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w14-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise an active challenge.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use an active challenge with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a water break and explain why it matters.","I can practise a thirst rating independently on a new prompt.","I can notice resting safely needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"An active water challenge links movement with hydration: do short bursts of activity, take water breaks and see how the body changes. Plan breaks before you feel very thirsty, drink slowly, stop if you feel dizzy or unwell and rest in the shade on hot days. A challenge can be…","options":["An active water challenge links movement with hydration: do short bursts of activity, take water breaks and see how the body changes. Plan breaks before you feel very thirsty, drink slowly, stop if you feel dizzy or unwell and rest in the shade on hot days. A challenge can be against yourself, such as keeping your own drink diary for a week, rather than against other people.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about an active challenge?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample challenge: four rounds of 2 minutes of activity (star jumps, jogging on the spot, hopping, marching), each followed by a 1 minute water break. Zoe rates her thirst from 1 to 5: 2 before, 4 after round 3, then 1 after drinking. She learns to drink before her thirst reaches a 4.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA challenge has the rule ''no water breaks until you have finished''. Rewrite the rule to make it safe and say why.\n\nFEEDBACK\nRewrite: ''Take a water break after every round, and anyone can stop to rest or drink at any time.'' Water breaks protect the body, and the choice to stop keeps the challenge safe and inclusive.","explain":"An active water challenge links movement with hydration: do short bursts of activity, take water breaks and see how the body changes. Plan breaks before you feel very thirsty, drink slowly, stop if you feel dizzy or unwell and rest in the shade on hot days. A challenge can be against yourself, such as keeping your own drink diary for a week, rather than against other people.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing the best athletes ignore thirst. Drinking regularly and resting are part of safe, healthy performance. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Activity and water breaks.","caption":"Drink every round.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Round</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">2 minutes of activity</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Break</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">1 minute of water</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example challenge: four rounds of 2 minutes of activity (star jumps, jogging on the spot, hopping, marching), each follo Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example challenge: four rounds of 2 minutes of activity (star jumps, jogging on the spot, hopping, marching), each follo Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about an active challenge?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample challenge: four rounds of 2 minutes of activity (star jumps, jogging on the spot, hopping, marching), each followed by a 1 minute water break. Zoe rates her thirst from 1 to 5: 2 before, 4 after round 3, then 1 after drinking. She learns to drink before her thirst reaches a 4. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Thirst ratings.","caption":"Drink before 4.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Thirst</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">2 before, 4 after</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Drink</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">back to 1</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about an active challenge?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving the best athletes ignore thirst. Drinking regularly and resting are part of safe, healthy performance. Here is a more helpful example: Example challenge: four rounds of 2 minutes of activity (star jumps, jogging on the spot, hopping, marching), each followed by a 1 minute water break. Zoe rates her thirst from 1 to 5: 2 before, 4 after round 3, then 1 after drinking. She learns to drink before her thirst reaches a 4.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nRewrite: ''Take a water break after every round, and anyone can stop to rest or drink at any time.'' Water breaks protect the body, and the choice to stop keeps the challenge safe and inclusive.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing the best athletes ignore thirst. Drinking regularly and resting are part of safe, healthy performance. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about an active challenge?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAn active water challenge links movement with hydration: do short bursts of activity, take water breaks and see how the body changes. Plan breaks before you feel very thirsty, drink slowly, stop if you feel dizzy or unwell and rest in the shade on hot days. A challenge can be against yourself, such as keeping your own drink diary for a week, rather than against other people. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 14, Lesson 2: Active water challenge","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Healthy food and nutrition","week":14,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample challenge: four rounds of 2 minutes of activity (star jumps, jogging on the spot, hopping, marching), each followed by a 1 minute water break. Zoe rates her thirst from 1 to 5: 2 before, 4 after round 3, then 1 after drinking. She learns to drink before her thirst reaches a 4.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Activity and water breaks.","caption":"Drink every round.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Round</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">2 minutes of activity</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Break</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">1 minute of water</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Thirst ratings.","caption":"Drink before 4.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Thirst</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">2 before, 4 after</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Drink</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">back to 1</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-pdhpe-y3-w14-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
